WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.540
Film Courage: I think you've said that the
word theme has been misused extensively when

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:10.470
it comes to film and television but I believe
you've also said that beware of the simple

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:11.470
one-liner?

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:14.440
Jeff Kitchen, Dramatist / Author / Founder
/ Consultant: Yeah it's a theme is something

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:19.539
that is misunderstood by a percentage of people.

00:00:19.539 --> 00:00:29.179
One of the things that my playwriting teacher
pointed out to me back when I was learning

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:35.730
this stuff and he explained it and then he
said this is really important make sure you

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:46.200
absorb this and he said the way in which the
protagonist resolves the dilemma expresses

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:55.839
the theme of the story and it's very clear
because the resolution is where the whole

00:00:55.839 --> 00:01:03.379
story comes together and the completion of
the story completes the magic spell on the

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:11.000
audience which crystallizes the mood that
they go home in like in a romantic comedy

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.570
it's been fun and fresh but also the romance
falling apart and things getting troubled

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:22.570
and then finally coming together in the end
and usually you feel happy and in love at

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:28.310
the end if it's done right and all of that
was skillfully orchestrated by the writer

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:37.990
and the filmmakers and the theme also comes
through that that which is being said organically

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:47.160
by the story comes together in the ending
and so if you look at the protagonist's act

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.500
of resolution of the dilemma and look at the
way in which the protagonist resolves the

00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.970
dilemma that enables you to put your finger
on the theme so for instance if you uh let's

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:14.640
say you know you know what if with our story
about Derek that say that he finds he creates

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:22.360
a third way out of the dilemma in which he's
neither trapped by the demands of his company

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:31.200
nor by the demands of the blackmailer or whatever
it ends up being and neither one is a good

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:39.970
way out and by thinking on his feet and maybe
working with Skylar he comes up with a creative

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:51.319
way to skate through this horrible trap that
he was in and so if you look at not you look

00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:57.260
at what he does but you look at the way in
which he does it it's about like thinking

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:02.760
creatively and realizing what he really needs
in life where he was always denying his own

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:10.340
needs and lying to himself and like that he's
finally brutally honest with himself and is

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:16.340
genuinely in love with her even though she
has betrayed him but she's also been a victim

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:23.569
and she's changed and he's grown and he's
able to synthesize a new life for himself

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:33.340
in the process of figuring out how to get
out of the trap that all of that is what the

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:39.040
story is about thematically it's about creating
a new life for yourself and finding a way

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:50.950
out of hell and powering out of a skid and
you can see that that creative type of resolution

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:57.180
of taking command of his life and powering
out of a bad situation is what the story is

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:06.520
about thematically and what's interesting
about being able to articulate the theme that's

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:16.309
emerging organically from a story is that
you often only start with a sense of it like

00:04:16.309 --> 00:04:27.500
in looking at that type of possible resolution
for derrick I can see it and sort of talk

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:32.430
around it and describe it in a bunch of different
ways like I just did like I just said it about

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:42.439
10 different ways but my my ability to articulate
it crisply and cleanly is not there yet because

00:04:42.439 --> 00:04:48.059
it you have to live with it for a while and

00:04:48.059 --> 00:04:54.310
look at what the audience leaves with they
leave with a sense of that theme in their

00:04:54.310 --> 00:05:00.740
gut in their soul and if you stop them on
the way out and ask them to articulate the

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:09.180
theme they probably couldn't they just have
it at a gut level it's in them so it has a

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:16.779
lot to do you want to be able to dwell with
a partially formed understanding of something

00:05:16.779 --> 00:05:26.310
as a crucial step in synthesizing your understanding
people are very uncomfortable with partial

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:33.930
solutions they always want to go no I need
to have the whole thing in my in a nutshell

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:39.659
so they don't like partial understanding it
kind of freaks people out but as a creative

00:05:39.659 --> 00:05:45.789
writer you need to get comfortable with that
because so often you're feeling your way through

00:05:45.789 --> 00:05:52.539
things where you're beginning to understand
it at a gut level but you have no capacity

00:05:52.539 --> 00:06:00.400
whatsoever at first for articulating it and
maybe you finally just got a handle on your

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:05.339
theme and your writing partner comes in the
room and you go like I got it I got it your

00:06:05.339 --> 00:06:08.449
writing partner was like so what is it what
is it spit it out and you're like I can't

00:06:08.449 --> 00:06:12.650
yet I got it leave me alone get out of my
face let me just sit here with it because

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:20.279
I got it and people are feeling creatures
that long before speech people just felt animals

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:28.849
do that were feeling creatures and so the
ability to

00:06:28.849 --> 00:06:34.361
look at the res look at the the protagonist's
resolution and look at the way in which he

00:06:34.361 --> 00:06:42.349
or she resolves the dilemma will put you right
on the theme that's actively emerging from

00:06:42.349 --> 00:06:48.570
organically in your story and it may be diametrically
opposed to what you thought the theme of your

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:55.039
story was going to be starting with a sense
of theme can often be troublesome because

00:06:55.039 --> 00:07:01.439
you can end up being preachy and trying to
dictate that to the viewers or the readers

00:07:01.439 --> 00:07:07.270
as opposed to that which organically emerges
as the story takes on a life of its own the

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:13.589
characters take on a life of their own and
you have to be able to if you're still stuck

00:07:13.589 --> 00:07:17.930
saying that the theme is what you set out
to have it be you may be the one that's missing

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:23.979
the boat the view if the viewers see that
movie they'll get it organically and carried

00:07:23.979 --> 00:07:29.800
away in their gut and think about it for a
while if it's deep and compelling and it may

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:34.669
affect how they interact with people for the
rest of their lives like they never yell at

00:07:34.669 --> 00:07:38.210
their kids again for the rest of their life
or something like that because something was

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:43.899
awakened in them even if they can't explain
it maybe maybe later they'll be able to but

00:07:43.899 --> 00:07:49.689
the important thing is that you changed them
through a complete the complete action of

00:07:49.689 --> 00:08:00.580
a compelling story so being able to have a
good strong sense of theme that's really emerging

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:11.159
from your story is critical because you as
the writer need to be able to be in touch

00:08:11.159 --> 00:08:17.740
with the theme that is coming through the
story if you're still stuck on what you thought

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:22.539
it was going to be then you're missing the
boat you're missing the chance to work with

00:08:22.539 --> 00:08:29.710
the real theme reinforce it utilize it and
so your story will tend to be weaker or you're

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:36.659
out of touch with what your story is actually
doing you know it's like you raise your kid

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:40.630
and my kid's going to be a doctor my kid's
going to be a doctor and you still see that

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:45.070
because you're intent on seeing that but if
you look at the reality of what your kid is

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:51.560
doing you'd be like he's a jazz musician holy
crap I thought he was a medical school but

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:59.319
no he is genuinely a jazz musician so it's
about what you want to see versus what's really

00:08:59.319 --> 00:09:11.139
there and theme is huge because you want to
be able to connect to the theme that's emerging

00:09:11.139 --> 00:09:18.350
from your story reasonably early in the process
you could see that with the derrick story

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:23.300
I went right to dilemma and I was all and
once I'm working with dilemma a bit I'm looking

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:28.940
ahead to crisis because that gives shape and
and focus to the theme the theme is going

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:34.329
to converge at the point of crisis and force
it is decision in action when there's like

00:09:34.329 --> 00:09:39.160
maybe 10 or 15 minutes left in the movie and
then the protagonist actively resolves the

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:47.630
dilemma once and for all that's the ending
of the story and then the way in which the

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:52.410
protagonist resolves the dilemma enables you
to put your finger on the theme that's actively

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:58.560
emerging through that story and you'll start
early in the development process of your story

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:03.900
but you've already got a tentative statement
or a tentative handle on your theme you want

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:10.589
to that's why I said beware of the clever
one-liner because if experts in creativity

00:10:10.589 --> 00:10:16.310
say there's often ten right answers to a question
and if you seize on one of those and say that's

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:24.690
the answer then you miss out on all the other
layers of complexity so again it comes back

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:32.470
to people's discomfort with a partially formed
understanding they it freaks them out to dwell

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:38.170
in that area where they sort of understand
it but not really but that's the bread and

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:48.500
butter of a writer where you're gradually
morphing through evolving ideas and once you

00:10:48.500 --> 00:11:01.860
can feel the theme and live with it and look
at all the aspects of your thought story through

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:11.990
the lens of your gradually evolving understanding
of the theme it will inform the whole story

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:17.170
because the theme really is what the story
is about it's that they call the governing

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:23.970
or a governing idea or the underlying philosophy
of the story or that which is being said by

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:29.569
the story and it may be again it may be the
opposite of what you set out to say or what

00:11:29.569 --> 00:11:38.790
you even want it to say but that's what's
really coming across to the audience and that's

00:11:38.790 --> 00:12:10.019
price said that the theme well let me read
you this quote from him yeah so this is price

00:12:10.019 --> 00:12:14.939
writing in 1908 about playwriting so I was
talking about plays but it's it's exactly

00:12:14.939 --> 00:12:21.899
the same stuff the highest form of play involves
the philosophy of life so that if the theme

00:12:21.899 --> 00:12:26.970
is not worth considering there is usually
little substance in a play with a theme your

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:32.759
play will be about something theme does not
stand alone no principle does mere theme will

00:12:32.759 --> 00:12:38.250
never make a play but it is a definite something
it furnishes a spiritual atmosphere and the

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:43.680
philosophy of the play it gives the clue to
the actual shaping of the play the tone depends

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:50.180
upon it it is the largest unit of the play
that's a very interesting statement that the

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:56.629
theme is the largest unit of the play that's
really interesting for instance the biggest

00:12:56.629 --> 00:13:09.920
organ in your body is your skin so it's that
kind of thing the theme is not infects the

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:15.759
whole story that's not the right word but
it's it's spread throughout the whole story

00:13:15.759 --> 00:13:23.240
it determines the shape and the tone of the
story and all that stuff so it's the ability

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:29.170
to come to grips with it is important and
gradually as you spend time living with it

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:36.009
your ability to articulate it will be enhanced
until it kind of crystallizes and then you're

00:13:36.009 --> 00:13:42.069
like oh yeah now I can explain it quite clearly
like Sydney Pollock could say the way that

00:13:42.069 --> 00:13:45.899
becoming a woman made a man out of Michael
Dorsey [TOOTSIE Movie] like there's a really

00:13:45.899 --> 00:13:51.839
crisp clear statement of what that story is
about thematically and it and it helped him

00:13:51.839 --> 00:13:58.040
so much and it gave a shape and a tone and
helped him communicate with the actors and

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:10.110
all that stuff so it's a very powerful aspect
of the story and the basics of it is you don't

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:15.939
want to rush it let yourself dwell with a
partially formed understanding of it and it

00:14:15.939 --> 00:14:20.570
will gradually gel and then crystallize but
not if you rush it.

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:21.570
